AB  - Smart-city Intelligent Transport Systems increasingly rely on low-latency data analytics that combine real measurements with data generated by Digital Twins. These systems require local data sovereignty and strong data retention control. Cloud-based traffic planning assistants may introduce privacy exposure, unpredictable response times, and Large Language Model bias, limiting their use in operational workflows. This paper presents an edge-resident traffic Digital Twin framework that (i) converts standard map representations into local knowledge graphs of road topology and traffic controls, (ii) implements a Graph Retrieval-Augmented Generation engine for topology-aware retrieval, and (iii) defines a modular software architecture for on-premise Large Language Model inference orchestration. We further introduce an unidirectional "Secure Edge Fusion"mechanism that ingests live traffic telemetry and compares it against twin results from simulation models. The system implements hybrid retrieval (vector + graph traversal), cross-encoder reranking, a two-tier cache for read-only queries, and entity-validation guardrails to prevent invalid references. An experimental edge implementation is tested across three urban scenarios, achieving cold-path turnaround averages of 1320 ms, and entity validation achieves 0% invalid-entity references, versus 12% for baseline. Results demonstrate a valid foundational framework for urban traffic Digital Twins. © 2026 IEEE.
